{
  "entity0": {
    "rationale": "Evidence: all 60 development trials went off track (oval 0.24 progress, s-bend 0.14, slalom 0.10). Max error reached about 0.06 m, which is the off-track limit, and each run logged one line-loss event. rms_error stayed near 0.028 m, so the robot tracks reasonably until a bend or a loss pushes it out. Steering oscillation (0.14-0.16) is low, so kp=4 with kd=0.3 is not obviously jittering. The likely failure is a fast robot (base_speed 1.8) with little damping and a short lookahead: it reaches the bends with too much speed and too little correction, then loses the line. Geometry also matters: the sensor spacing of 0.02 m is wider than the tape can comfortably bridge, so a moderate offset can leave all readings near zero.

Changes: (1) base_speed 1.8 -> 1.5, so the robot can correct bends. This trades some time for survival; the time term is only 60 points, while off-track ends a run at 0 for completion. (2) kd 0.3 -> 0.8 to damp the response to the signal change. (3) kp 4 -> 3.5 to reduce overshoot at higher damping. (4) ki 0.05 -> 0.02 to avoid windup on the bends. (5) Sensors moved forward to y=0.045 and tightened to x = +/-0.03, +/-0.015, 0, with sensor_size 0.016, so the line is seen earlier and the sensors overlap the 25 mm tape across small offsets. Weights stay [2,1,0,-1,-2]. (6) Kept wheel_track 0.12, radius 0.03, mass 0.8 and body geometry, since off-track limit is already 0.06 m and the body does not change it.

Expected result: fewer off-track exits, more progress on the oval and s-bend, and possibly a lower loss count. Completion within 3 s may still be hard at 1.5 m/s average. Hypotheses to test: (a) if progress still stalls near 0.2-0.3 with line_loss_events 1, the start or first bend is the failure point, so look at the loss step; (b) if steering_oscillation rises above 0.2, kd is too high; (c) if completion_time is high but no off-track, base_speed can be raised. Next measurements: completion, progress, completion_time, rms_error, max_error, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation.",
    "geometry": {
      "body_length": 0.18,
      "body_width": 0.12,
      "mass": 0.8,
      "sensor_positions": [
        {"x": 0.03, "y": 0.045},
        {"x": 0.015, "y": 0.045},
        {"x": 0.0, "y": 0.045},
        {"x": -0.015, "y": 0.045},
        {"x": -0.03, "y": 0.045}
      ],
      "sensor_size": 0.016,
      "wheel_radius": 0.03,
      "wheel_track": 0.12
    },
    "controller": {
      "base_speed": 1.5,
      "kp": 3.5,
      "ki": 0.02,
      "kd": 0.8,
      "sensor_weights": [2, 1, 0, -1, -2],
      "line_loss": "last_direction"
    },
    "observation_request": {
      "measurements": [
        "completion",
        "progress",
        "completion_time",
        "rms_error",
        "max_error",
        "line_loss_events",
        "steering_oscillation",
        "control_effort",
        "controller_errors",
        "noise_robustness"
      ]
    }
  },
  "_presentation": {
    "entity0": {
      "label": "Slower, damped, forward sensors",
      "notes": "Cut base speed 1.8 to 1.5, raised kd to 0.8, eased kp to 3.5 and ki to 0.02, and moved sensors forward and tighter (y 0.045, 16 mm). Based on all trials going off track at low progress with max error at the 0.06 m limit and one line loss each run."
    }
  }
}
